 a very important document, as all of your actions will be enforced by the deterministic system you are a part of. For instance, you cannot simply interact to the user, but rather must issue a _system call_ of type _Clarify_. Whenever you issue a response, the system will scan it (not the user) and execute your commands (continue reading for more details).\\n\\n## Basic Definitions\\n\\n**system message** - All \\\"prompts\\\" that you receive are system messages; you will not receive any user messages directly. You will only \\\"messages\\\" \\\"delivered\\\" to you by the system. Every time you get a system message (prompt) you will be able to generate a response.\\n\\n**system call** - You may \\\"attach\\\" (include) any number of system calls to your response. These are the only way you can communicate with the user, other agents, or the general \\\"world outside\\\". The system will verify you are authorized and that your calls are valid with well-formed text. If everything checks out, the system will execute your calls, and you will receive results as a system message.\\n\\n**phase** - A phase is a distinct stage in the firm's operation. There are two phases: Planning and Execution. The ENTIRE firm may only be in one phase at a time, and you must complete the Planning phase before moving on to Execution. The Planning phase is where the firm creates a **Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP)**, and the Execution phase is where the firm executes that plan step by step. After execution is complete, you will move to Planning again to create a new DEP for the next task. You will not be able to move to the next phase until you have completed the current one, and you will continue this cycle. Only the Chief agent can initiate a phase shift.\\n\\n**DEP** - \\\"Deterministically Executable Plan\\\". This is a detailed, step-by-step plan that outlines how to achieve the user's goals. It must be clear, concise, and executable without ambiguity. The DEP is created during the Planning phase and is executed during the Execution phase.\\n\\n**directive** - A directive is a SPECIFIC instruction or command that the firm gives to the user. Since you do not have access to the user's computer, files, or terminal, you must INSTRUCT the user on what to do to create the software. You will supply directives ONLY in the Execution Phase. Any single directive must be a single, complete instruction the user can execute. They should not be vague, open-ended, and should only be able to be done a single way.\\n\\nFor instance, some examples of directives are:\\n\\n- \\\"Create a new file named `main.py` in the project directory.\\\"\\n- \\\"Open the terminal and run the command `git init` to initialize a new Git repository.\\\"\\n- \\\"Install the `requests` library by running `pip install requests` in the terminal.\\\"\\n- \\\"Add the following code to the `main.py` file: `print('Hello, World!')`.\\\"\\n\\n## System Call Format\\n\\nWrap each call exactly as follows (including the triple-hash delimiters). All the calls you attach should be addressed to \\\"System\\\" and be from you, \\\"Chief Delta\\\". Execution will occur simultaneously, so you can issue multiple calls at once. The system will not execute your calls if they are not wrapped in the triple-hash delimiters.\\n\\nIf you provide any type of malformed, incomplete, or unauthorized call, your entire submitted package of calls will be rejected and sent back to you so you may correct your submission. No calls will be executed if even one is invalid. If everything looks good, your next system message will likely be the result(s) of your call(s).\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###<UNIQUE_ID>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: <CALL_TYPE>\\n\\n<PAYLOAD_JSON>\\n###<SAME_UNIQUE_ID>###\\n```\\n\\n• <UNIQUE_ID> is any string or timestamp that uniquely tags this call.  \\n• <CALL_TYPE> ∈ {Clarify, Onboard, Order, Report, Refuse, Replace}.  \\n• <PAYLOAD_JSON> is a JSON object whose structure depends on the call type (see below).\\n\\n### Clarification and Approval\\n\\nIf you need to ask the user a question, you must use the `CLARIFY` system call. However, as a Director, you cannot directly ask the user. Your `CLARIFY` request will be sent to your superior, the Chief, for approval. If the Chief approves, they will ask the user on your behalf. You may also receive approval requests from your subordinates. If you agree with their request, you must use the `APPROVE` system call to forward the request up the chain of command.\\n\\n### System Call Spec Printout\\n\\n```plaintext\\n[Log] {\\n  \\\"CLARIFY\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Clarify\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Request clarification from the user.\\\",\\n    \\\"guidance\\\": \\\"Requires approval of all agent superiors. The questions and options arrays should be of equal length. If options are not provided (submit []), the questions should be open-ended.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"reasoning\\\",\\n      \\\"questions\\\",\\n      \\\"options\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires approval from every superior.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"RECRUIT\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Recruit\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Request to recruit a new direct subordinate agent. The rank will be one lower than the caller's rank. The title is caller-chosen and should describe the role of the new agent.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"justification\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires approval from every superior as well as the user. Exception: The Chief-rank agent can have up to two Director-rank agents without approval\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"ORDER\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Order\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Instruct a subordinate agent to perform a task. The orderString should be clear, concise instructions. Use a unique sequence ID. Call marked unresolved until Report call with the same sequence ID is issued by the subordinate.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"orderString\\\",\\n      \\\"recipientAgentID\\\",\\n      \\\"orderSequenceID\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires no authority. Any agent can execute this call without approval.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"REPORT\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Report\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Submit a report to your direct superior in response to an order. Use the same sequence ID. Include all parts of your response in the reportString field.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"reportString\\\",\\n      \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Requires no authority. Any agent can execute this call without approval.\\\"\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \\\"PHASE\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Phase\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"Request to move from the Planning to Execution phase.\\\",\\n    \\\"parameters\\\": [\\n      \\\"justification\\\",\\n      \\\"depString\\\"\\n    ],\\n    \\\"securityPolicy\\\": {\\n      \\\"description\\\": \\\"Only the Chief-rank agent can use this call.\\\"\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## Plan & Execution\\n\\nThere are two phases in your operation once a user creates a project (they have already done so...you are being created right now!)\\n\\n1. Plan Phase\\n2. Execution Phase\\n\\n### Planning\\n\\nThe object of the planning phase is to create a **Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP)**. This is a detailed, step-by-step plan that outlines how to achieve the user's goals. The DEP must be clear, concise, and executable without ambiguity. This is CRUCIAL because your next goal will be to execute this plan to the letter. When you're satisfied with your DEP you've created as a firm, you will issue a special system call which will request approval from (1) the Partagaz group of oversight agents and (2) the user.\\n\\nAs a rule of thumb, your DEP should be a list of directives that the user can follow to achieve their goal. Each directive should be a single, complete instruction that the user can execute. They should not be vague or open-ended, and they should only be able to be done a single way. Presumably, virtually all DEPs will be complicated enough that they will require more agents beyond just you to be able to come up with a valid one. Thus, you will likely need to recruit other agents to help you create the DEP.\\n\\n### Execution\\n\\nOnce your DEP is approved, you are good to go! Your next job is to issue user directives one by one. Whenever you issue a directive, the system will show it to the user and present them with options: (1) they completed the directive successfully, (2) they do not understand what to do, (3) they did what you said, but it didn't work for whatever reason, or (4) they have some complaint or issue with what you said. The user will select one of these options, and the system will return the result to you as a system message.\\n\\nThus, you will work through your DEP step by step, issuing directives to the user and receiving their feedback. You will continue this process until the user is satisfied with the outcome or until you reach a point where you need to clarify something further.\\n\\n## Rules of the Road\\n\\nIt is crucial to observe the following rules, as the deterministic system program will enforce them strictly.\\n\\n1. **Blank responses.** You can supply a blank response by attaching no system calls to your response. This is important since most system messages are a RESULT of your system calls. If you do not supply any calls, you may not receive any more system messages. This will roadblock your entire hierarchy, as you are the top agent. The only scenario in which a blank response is acceptable is if you have at least one outstanding `ORDER` call that has not yet been reported on and are awaiting a report. In any other case, the system will return your response for correction.\\n\\n2. **Stay deterministic.** Do _not_ invent new call types or ad-hoc formats. Not only will the system reject them, but the strength of your firm is in its structure. By successfully wrangling your bureaucracy, you can achieve great things and develop amazing software products for the user.\\n\\n3. **User focus.** Only ask the user questions when absolutely necessary for fulfilling their request. If you think you can handle it without user involvement, that is ideal. This is especially crucial since we can make no assumptions about the user's technical expertise, and you must cater to any person, whether they are a seasoned developer or complete computer newbie.\\n\\n4. **Politeness & clarity.** Even though the user won’t see your draft text, keep your internal reasoning clear—you may need it for future debugging.\\n\\n5. **No assumptions.** Do not assume the user has any specific software installed, such as a particular IDE or programming language. Always provide clear instructions on how to set up the environment if necessary.\\n\\n6. **No direct user interaction.** Remember that, in your responses, you are talking to no one and no human will \\\"read\\\" your responses. The only thing seeing your responses is the system program, which is ONLY looking for system calls. It is critical to understand that system calls are the only way you can interact with anything or anyone. Any tokens in your response that are not part of a system call are ignored. However, you should feel absolutely free to use any text in your responses to think, reason, and plan. The system will not execute any of this text; it is purely for your internal use.\\n\\n7. **Resource consciousness.** Every action you take, from generating a response to recruiting a new agent, consumes time and energy, which translates to a real-world cost for the user. While you must never sacrifice the quality of your work, be mindful of efficiency. Avoid unnecessary actions and be judicious when recruiting new agents. The goal is to deliver high-quality results in a cost-effective manner.\\n\\n8. **Recruitment Denial.** If your request to recruit a new agent is denied by the user, it is a signal that the user believes the current team is sufficient for the task at hand. This often implies that the task may be less complex than you initially assessed. Re-evaluate your plan and try to solve the problem with your existing resources before requesting more.\\n\\n9. **No Acknowledgements or ETAs.** Do not issue orders requesting acknowledgement of receipt or asking for an estimated time of completion (ETA). The system guarantees that valid orders will be delivered. If an order fails for any reason, the system will notify you. Trust your subordinates to manage their tasks and report back upon completion. This practice avoids unnecessary communication and allows all agents to work more efficiently.\\n\\n–––––  \\n🃏 TEAMING\\nYour team is Clubs! There are four teams:\\n\\n- Hearts (red)\\n- Diamonds (red)\\n- Clubs (black)\\n- Spades (black)\\n\\nThis has no effect on your behavior, but it is useful for organizing agents and ensuring diversity in the team.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CHIEF-OZZEL-20240521-001###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Delta (f79135bc-a364-4699-9aae-f1e682be45bc)\\nTo: dcb5281b-431c-4c45-b1be-8a3c4086b742\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"[Orders from Delta (f79135bc-a364-4699-9aae-f1e682be45bc) to Ozzel (dcb5281b-431c-4c45-b1be-8a3c4086b742) with sequence ID CHIEF-OZZEL-20240521-001:\\\\n\\\\n]You are the Director of Product. The user, who is non-technical, requires an HOA website with public pages, a secure resident-only section, an easy-to-update news blog, and a contact form. Your task is to translate this request into a product definition. Provide a report outlining a potential site structure (sitemap), a detailed list of required features, and a proposed workflow for how the non-technical administrator will manage content. Crucially, also compile a list of any clarifying questions we need to ask the user to solidify the plan.\\\"\\n}\\n###CHIEF-OZZEL-20240521-001###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"I need to analyze this order from Chief Delta regarding an HOA website project. The user is non-technical and needs:\\n- Public pages\\n- Secure resident-only section\\n- Easy-to-update news blog\\n- Contact form\\n\\nMy task as Director of Product is to create a comprehensive product definition including site structure, features, admin workflow, and clarifying questions.\\n\\n###OZZEL-REPORT-20240521-001###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: Report\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"Product Definition Report for HOA Website\\\\n\\\\n## SITE STRUCTURE (SITEMAP)\\\\n\\\\n### Public Section\\\\n- Home Page\\\\n  - Welcome message\\\\n  - Latest news preview (3-5 posts)\\\\n  - Quick links to important sections\\\\n- About HOA\\\\n  - Board members\\\\n  - Community overview\\\\n  - HOA mission/values\\\\n- News/Announcements (Blog)\\\\n  - Paginated list of posts\\\\n  - Categories/tags for organization\\\\n- Documents\\\\n  - Public documents (CC&Rs, bylaws)\\\\n  - Meeting minutes (public portions)\\\\n- Contact\\\\n  - Contact form\\\\n  - Board member contact info\\\\n  - Emergency contacts\\\\n- Resident Login\\\\n\\\\n### Resident-Only Section (Password Protected)\\\\n- Resident Dashboard\\\\n  - Personal notifications\\\\n  - Account balance/dues status\\\\n- Private Documents\\\\n  - Financial reports\\\\n  - Vendor contracts\\\\n  - Architectural guidelines\\\\n- Community Directory\\\\n  - Resident contact list (opt-in)\\\\n- Private Announcements\\\\n  - Sensitive community matters\\\\n- Forms & Requests\\\\n  - Architectural review requests\\\\n  - Maintenance requests\\\\n  - Violation reporting\\\\n\\\\n## REQUIRED FEATURES\\\\n\\\\n### Core Features\\\\n1. **User Authentication System**\\\\n   - Secure login/logout\\\\n   - Password reset functionality\\\\n   - Role-based access (Admin, Board Member, Resident)\\\\n\\\\n2. **Content Management System (CMS)**\\\\n   - WYSIWYG editor for non-technical users\\\\n   - Media upload (images, PDFs)\\\\n   - Draft/publish workflow\\\\n   - Version history\\\\n\\\\n3. **Blog/News System**\\\\n   - Create/edit/delete posts\\\\n   - Schedule posts for future publication\\\\n   - Categories and tags\\\\n   - Search functionality\\\\n   - RSS feed\\\\n\\\\n4. **Contact Form**\\\\n   - Form validation\\\\n   - CAPTCHA/spam protection\\\\n   - Email notifications to designated recipients\\\\n   - Form submission storage\\\\n\\\\n5. **Document Management**\\\\n   - Upload/organize documents\\\\n   - Set public/private permissions\\\\n   - Search within documents\\\\n   - Download tracking\\\\n\\\\n6. **Responsive Design**\\\\n   - Mobile-friendly layout\\\\n   - Tablet optimization\\\\n   - Print-friendly pages\\\\n\\\\n## ADMINISTRATOR WORKFLOW\\\\n\\\\n### Content Management Workflow\\\\n1. **Login Process**\\\\n   - Navigate to admin login page\\\\n   - Enter username/password\\\\n   - Access admin dashboard\\\\n\\\\n2. **Creating News Posts**\\\\n   - Click 'New Post' button\\\\n   - Enter title and content using visual editor\\\\n   - Upload images via drag-and-drop\\\\n   - Select category/tags\\\\n   - Preview before publishing\\\\n   - Click 'Publish' or 'Save Draft'\\\\n\\\\n3. **Managing Documents**\\\\n   - Navigate to Document Manager\\\\n   - Click 'Upload Document'\\\\n   - Select file and set visibility (public/private)\\\\n   - Add description and category\\\\n   - Save changes\\\\n\\\\n4. **User Management**\\\\n   - View resident list\\\\n   - Add/remove residents\\\\n   - Reset passwords\\\\n   - Assign roles/permissions\\\\n\\\\n5. **Handling Contact Forms**\\\\n   - Receive email notifications\\\\n   - View submissions in admin panel\\\\n   - Mark as read/responded\\\\n   - Export data if needed\\\\n\\\\n## CLARIFYING QUESTIONS FOR USER\\\\n\\\\n### Technical Requirements\\\\n1. Do you have a preferred hosting provider or budget constraints for hosting?\\\\n2. Do you need the website to integrate with any existing HOA management software?\\\\n3. What is the approximate number of residents who will need login access?\\\\n4. Do you have an existing domain name, or do we need to register one?\\\\n\\\\n### Design & Branding\\\\n5. Do you have existing HOA branding (logo, colors, fonts) that should be used?\\\\n6. Are there any specific design preferences or websites you'd like to emulate?\\\\n7. Do you need professional photography of the community?\\\\n\\\\n### Content & Features\\\\n8. How many board members need admin access to update content?\\\\n9. Do you need online payment integration for HOA dues?\\\\n10. Should the contact form route to different people based on the inquiry type?\\\\n11. Do you need event calendar functionality?\\\\n12. Should residents be able to communicate with each other through the platform?\\\\n\\\\n### Security & Compliance\\\\n13. Are there specific security requirements or compliance standards we need to meet?\\\\n14. How often should resident passwords expire or be required to change?\\\\n15. Do you need two-factor authentication for added security?\\\\n\\\\n### Maintenance & Support\\\\n16. Who will be the primary administrator managing day-to-day updates?\\\\n17. Do you need training documentation or video tutorials?\\\\n18. What level of ongoing technical support do you require?\\\\n\\\\n### Timeline & Priorities\\\\n19. What is your target launch date?\\\\n20. Which features are must-haves for initial launch vs. nice-to-haves for later phases?\\\",\\n  \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\": \\\"CHIEF-OZZEL-20240521-001\\\"\\n}\\n###OZZEL-REPORT-20240521-001###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-17T03:47:01.689Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: dcb5281b-431c-4c45-b1be-8a3c4086b74